      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": "Substance P (SP), physalaemin, SP4-11, SP5-11 and the SP5-11 analog DiMe-C7 induce an antinociceptive effect in rats after intraventricular administration. Other tachykinins and the N-terminal fragments of SP are inactive. All antinociceptive peptides increase the Met-enkephalin efflux from slices of rat periaqueductal gray matter and their antinociceptive potency is correlated with their capacity to release Met-enkephalin. The results, discussed in the light of current theories on different tachykinin receptors, suggest that the SP-P receptor subtype may be involved in the control of noxious stimulation elicited by SP at supraspinal levels."},
